   --- §26.6 (E.2 cites it for the accelerator identity and Richardson) L7172–L7193
   L7172 ### 26.6 Aitken's Δ² has an exact bias on a Rydberg series
   L7173  Aitken's process extrapolates a sequence to its limit. A Rydberg series converges to its
   L7174  ionisation limit. So apply it.
   L7175 
   L7176 | *n* | *T*(*n*) | Aitken(*T*) | *T*/3 |
   L7177 |---|---|---|---|
   L7178 | 10 | 1097.3730 | **365.1793** | 365.7910 |
   L7179 | 20 | 274.3433 | **91.4096** | 91.4477 |
   L7180 | 40 | 68.5858 | **22.8596** | 22.8619 |
   L7181 | 80 | 17.1465 | **5.7153** | 5.7155 |
   L7182 
   L7183       Aitken does not reach the limit. It reaches T/3.
   L7184 
   L7185  **Because the correction it subtracts is (Δ*T*)²/Δ²*T* → *T*′²/*T*″ = (2/3)*T* — exactly λ² of §23.11 — leaving (1/3)*T*.** Aitken's kernel is *geometric* conv
   L7186 
   L7187  **With a practical consequence.** Aitken's Δ² is a standard tool for estimating a limit from measured terms. **On a Rydberg series it lands at *I* − *T*/3, det
   L7188 
   L7189       Not a better estimate. A stated, exact bias in a standard one — which is what this book's
   L7190       method is for.
   L7191 
   L7192  **And λ² now carries three readings across three fields:** the decrease obtained by a full Newton step · the correction Aitken subtracts · **two-thirds of the 
   L7193 
